WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
How do you read the order of bases along a DNA strand?

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.340
Sanger sequencing turns that challenge into sorting by length.

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.020
It copies the strand, stopping different copies at every base.

4
00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:21.453
Start with a single-stranded template and a primer, a short DNA strand paired to it.

5
00:00:22.453 --> 00:00:28.293
The primer gives the copying enzyme, polymerase, a 3' end, where new nucleotides

6
00:00:28.293 --> 00:00:34.593
join. Add ordinary nucleotides plus a little chain-stopping dideoxynucleotide.

7
00:00:35.953 --> 00:00:41.073
Each stopper keeps a normal base and 5' triphosphate, so polymerase adds it.

8
00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:44.967
A dideoxynucleotide stops a copy for good.

9
00:00:46.047 --> 00:00:50.347
It lacks the 3' hydroxyl the next nucleotide must bond to.

10
00:00:51.547 --> 00:00:56.347
At each site most copies take an ordinary nucleotide, and a few take a stopper.

11
00:00:57.567 --> 00:00:59.247
So stops fall at every position.

12
00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:05.867
Stopping copies at known bases turns DNA order into a ladder of fragment lengths.

13
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.380
Next, sort the stopped copies by length.

14
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:14.540
Classically, four tubes each held one stopper type, or terminator.

15
00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:19.660
Four gel lanes separated pieces differing by one nucleotide.

16
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:26.280
Later, each terminator got its own fluorescent dye, so one reaction holds all four.

17
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.740
A capillary sorts fragments by size while a detector reads each colour.

18
00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.430
Now a direction puzzle.

19
00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:41.950
Suppose a detector reports the shortest fragments in order as A, C, and G. What are

20
00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:45.450
the new strand and its template, each written 5' to 3'?

21
00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:56.653
The copy grows from the primer at its 3' end, so the shortest fragment holds the

22
00:01:56.653 --> 00:02:02.393
first new base. The new strand reads 5'-ACG-3'.

23
00:02:03.213 --> 00:02:09.573
Its antiparallel template pairs opposite as 3'-TGC-5'.

24
00:02:10.243 --> 00:02:13.513
Written 5' to 3', that is CGT.

25
00:02:14.813 --> 00:02:18.213
Complementing without reversing reads the template backwards.

26
00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:21.787
Sanger reads are accurate, but limited.

27
00:02:23.127 --> 00:02:28.227
Each reaction reads one region, commonly up to about 800 to 1,000 bases.

28
00:02:29.747 --> 00:02:35.047
Because the first few dozen bases are often poor, experimenters cannot rely on the

29
00:02:35.047 --> 00:02:41.267
start. And reporting bases is not locating them across repeated genome segments.

30
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:49.570
In 1977, Sanger, Nicklen and Coulson published chain termination, using DNA from the

31
00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:56.850
phage phiX174. It became the basis of most DNA sequencing for the next quarter

32
00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:03.770
century. In 1980, Sanger shared half of the Chemistry Nobel with Walter Gilbert, his

33
00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:04.770
second Nobel.

34
00:03:05.483 --> 00:03:10.843
Fluorescent capillary Sanger chemistry produced most of the sequence in the February

35
00:03:10.843 --> 00:03:13.603
2001 human genome drafts.

36
00:03:14.803 --> 00:03:20.163
The International Human Genome Sequencing Consortium and Celera Genomics each

37
00:03:20.163 --> 00:03:27.823
published one. The consortium's finished sequence of 2004 covered about 99% % of the

38
00:03:27.823 --> 00:03:30.363
gene-rich euchromatin, still with gaps.

39
00:03:30.363 --> 00:03:35.523
One missing hydroxyl turned DNA order into readable lengths.
